tion; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine with one aldehyde, and intramolecular capture of the second aldehyde followed by dehydration furnishes the five-membered cyclic imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"1,4-Dialdehydes are strongly predisposed to form five-membered N-heterocycles with ammonia. This step efficiently builds the pyrroline ring that underlies proline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct cyclic Strecker capture of succinaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"HCN/NH3-rich pool; mildly basic; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Succinaldehyde and ammonia generate a cyclic iminium in situ that is trapped by cyanide to give a cyclic aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"HCN and NH3 are efficient prebiotic reagents for converting carbonyls into aminonitriles. Because succinaldehyde can first cyclize, cyanide trapping gives a direct proline-skeleton precursor in a single branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide addition to 1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"neutral to mildly basic pool chemistry; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic cyanide adds to the iminium carbon of 1-pyrroline; protonation gives pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Cyclic imines are activated electrophiles, so cyanide additio