yde and acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["montmorillonite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "montmorillonite", "aqueous": "true", "other": "slightly alkaline evaporitic pool; wet-dry concentration; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Clay-assisted enolization of ethanal followed by attack on formaldehyde gives the beta-hydroxy aldehyde 3-hydroxypropanal.", "reasoning": "Formaldehyde lacks alpha hydrogens and serves mainly as an electrophile, so crossed aldol chemistry with acetaldehyde can bias formation toward the C3 beta-hydroxy aldehyde rather than self-aldol products. Dry-down concentration and clay surfaces both favor this step in surface pools."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Dehydration of 3-hydroxypropanal to acrolein", "inputs": ["mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["silica"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "silica", "aqueous": "false", "other": "repeated drying on hot mineral surfaces; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Mineral-surface elimination of water from the beta-hydroxy aldehyde forms the conjugated enal acrolein.", "reasoning": "Beta-hydroxy aldehydes dehydrate readily during dry-down, and acrolein is a plausible product of concentrated hydroxyaldehydes in geothermal or evaporitic settings. This provides a compact route from C1 and C2 carbonyl feedstocks to a reactive C3 enal."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Hydrothermal hydroformylation-like conversion of acrolein to succinaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["awaruite (Ni3Fe)", "mackinawite (FeS)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "380-450K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Ni-Fe alloy/sulfide surfaces", "aqueous": "true", "other": "H2-rich vent fluids, CO-bearing, moderate alkalinity, anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Acrolein adsorbs on Ni/Fe sites, CO inserts 