{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T13:59:23.706910", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: L-Proline\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.35\n- Feedback: The graph is connected, has sufficient nodes, and molecule IDs are used consistently, but network validity is substantially weakened because 8 of 12 reactions fail the deterministic balance check. Repairable bookkeeping omissions include rxn_002 (+H2O product), rxn_003 (+H2 reactant, with water bookkeeping also needing revision), rxn_007 (+H2O reactant), rxn_008 (+H2O reactant and NH3 product), rxn_009 (+2 H2O reactants and NH3 product), and rxn_011 (+H2 reactant). More serious, rxn_004 and rxn_005 do not conserve mass as written and likely misidentify the actual cyclization products or omit essential redox steps. Environmental consistency is only moderate because transfers between surface, hydrothermal, aqueous biochemical, and final mineral-resolution settings are not physically justified, and encoding hydrogen equivalents only in conditions is not sufficient for a stoichiometric network. The DL-proline -> L-proline step should be represented as an explicit resolution with stated ee and recovery, not a full chemical conversion. Best current route is P8, but no pathway is close to approval.\n- Issues: ['Conceptually coherent from the C1/C2 branch, but rxn_002 must release H2O, rxn_003 needs explicit H2 and better water bookkeeping, rxn_005 is not conserved as written and likely misstates the direct cyclic Strecker product or saturation state, and rxn_009 should include 2 H2O as reactants with NH3 as product. rxn_012 is only an enantioenrichment or resolution step, so L-proline is not convincingly obtained in high yield.', 'The amide relay is more chemically standard than direct one-step nitrile hydrolysis, but rxn_005 remains the main weakness because the direct cyclic Strecker step is not conserved and likely has the wrong product identity. rxn_007 needs H2O as reactant, rxn_008 needs H2O as reactant with NH3 release, and the pathway still depends on speculative rxn_003. Transfers between surface, hydrothermal, aqueous biochemical, and final mineral settings are not justified.', 'This is the strongest internal route because rxn_001 and rxn_006 are plausible. However, rxn_002 still needs H2O as product, rxn_003 is a speculative hydrothermal hydroformylation-like step that should explicitly consume H2, and rxn_004 is chemically problematic because succinaldehyde plus NH3 does not give 1-pyrroline as written without additional redox or different products. rxn_009 again needs 2 H2O in and NH3 out, and rxn_012 only resolves the racemate.', 'Mechanistically coherent after 1-pyrroline formation, but the pathway is still bottlenecked by flawed rxn_004. The hydrolysis relay is under-specified: rxn_007 requires H2O, and rxn_008 requires H2O with NH3 release. rxn_003 remains speculative, and movement between surface, hydrothermal, aqueous biochemical, and final mineral-resolution environments is not justified.', 'The noncyanide branch avoids nitrile hydrolysis, but it still depends on broken rxn_004. rxn_010 is balanced but highly speculative as a prebiotic metal-assisted carboxylation of 1-pyrroline to P5C, and rxn_011 needs explicit H2. The final L-proline claim remains weak because rxn_012 is a resolution step, not asymmetric synthesis.', 'This route is shorter, but shortening does not solve the main chemistry. rxn_003 is speculative and under-balanced, rxn_005 is not mass-conserving and likely has the wrong product or saturation state, rxn_009 needs 2 H2O and NH3 bookkeeping, and rxn_012 is only an enrichment step rather than a demonstrated conversion to isolated L-proline.', 'The amide relay is more defensible than direct one-step nitrile hydrolysis, but the route still hinges on mis-specified rxn_005. rxn_007 should include H2O, rxn_008 should include H2O and release NH3, and the biochemical environment label is too vague to assess compatibility with cyanide chemistry and hydrolysis.', 'Best current branch. rxn_006 is chemically reasonable, and rxn_009 can be made standard by adding 2 H2O as reactants and NH3 as product. However, the pathway is still limited by speculative rxn_003 and especially rxn_004, which does not conserve mass and likely misidentifies the direct ammonia cyclization product. The final L-proline step is still only a mineral resolution.', 'Close to P8, and the amide relay is chemically standard once corrected, but the branch still inherits the major problem at rxn_004. Add H2O to rxn_007, add H2O and NH3 bookkeeping to rxn_008, and justify movement between hydrothermal, aqueous biochemical, and mineral-surface settings.', 'The P5C concept is coherent in outline, but the actual chemistry is weak as specified. rxn_004 is still broken, rxn_010 is speculative without strong prebiotic precedent, rxn_011 needs explicit H2, and rxn_012 is only a resolution. This branch is the least supported mechanistically.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-Proline\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The network converges on the C4 cyclic imine 1-pyrroline as a chemically central hub. Two feedstock families are used on geochemical merit alone in the same broad surface-geothermal setting: (i) succinaldehyde undergoing H2-driven reductive amination/cyclization, and (ii) acrolein undergoing hydrocyanation to 4-oxobutanenitrile followed by hydrogenation to 4-aminobutanal. From 1-pyrroline, the C5 proline skeleton is completed by either CO2 carboxylation to \u03941-pyrroline-2-carboxylate or Strecker-type HCN capture followed by hydrolysis. Because these routes are not intrinsically enantioselective on achiral mineral surfaces, the network explicitly produces racemic proline first and then isolates an L-enriched fraction by a separate mineral-assisted resolution step, rather than incorrectly treating L-proline as if it were formed directly in bulk.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"molecular hydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"prop-2-enal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acrolein\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O/c1-2-3-4/h2-3H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-oxobutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": null,\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H5NO/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h4H,1-2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"gamma-aminobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H9NO/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h4H,1-3,5H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"butane-1,4-dial\",\n      \"common_name\": \"succinaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H6O2/c5-3-1-2-4-6/h3-4H,1-2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H7N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3,4,5-tetrahydro-1H-pyrrole\",\n      \"common_name\": \"1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"3,4-dihydro-2H-pyrrole-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"\u03941-pyrroline-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"proline nitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-proline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2S)-pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"L-proline\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO2/c7-5(8)4-2-1-3-6-4/h4,6H,1-3H2,(H,7,8)/t4-/m0/s1\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrocyanation of acrolein to 4-oxobutanenitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly basic cyanide-bearing pool; conjugate addition favored over prolonged UV exposure.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the \u03b2-carbon of the enal, followed by proton transfer to give the Michael-type adduct 4-oxobutanenitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acrolein is an activated \u03b1,\u03b2-unsaturated aldehyde and is a reasonable cyanide acceptor in surface brines. Writing the step as simple hydrocyanation avoids the earlier under-specified bookkeeping problems and gives a mass-balanced C4 nitrile-aldehyde intermediate.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogenation of 4-oxobutanenitrile to 4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiS\", \"FeS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiS/FeS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"H2-rich geothermal seep or subaerial geothermal field; aldehyde chemoselectivity is likely imperfect.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-mediated hydrogenation of the nitrile to the corresponding primary amine gives the amino aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step is chemically coherent and fully balanced because H2 is explicit. It is also one of the weaker links in the network: selective nitrile reduction in the presence of an aldehyde is difficult, so the branch should be regarded as lower-confidence than the succinaldehyde routes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"One-pot hydrocyanation and hydrogenation of acrolein to 4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiS\", \"FeS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiS/FeS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"H2-rich geothermal pool where the nitrile intermediate need not accumulate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net combination of cyanide conjugate addition to acrolein followed by mineral-assisted hydrogenation of the nitrile to the amino aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This reaction represents the same chemistry as rxn_001 plus rxn_002 under continuous one-pot conditions. It is intentionally marked as an alternative overall route rather than a preferred step, and it remains speculative for the same chemoselectivity reasons as rxn_002.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclization of 4-aminobutanal to 1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentration by evaporation or at mineral interfaces shifts the amino aldehyde/imine equilibrium toward the cyclic imine.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular attack of the amino group on the aldehyde forms a hemiaminal that dehydrates to the five-membered cyclic imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The 4-aminobutanal/1-pyrroline pair is a standard cyclization equilibrium, and this explicit dehydration step fixes the bookkeeping that was previously missing around amino-aldehyde cyclization.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive cyclization of succinaldehyde and ammonia to 1-pyrroline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiS\", \"FeS\", \"native Fe\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiS/FeS/native Fe\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"H2-rich geothermal field with wet-dry concentration cycles.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"One aldehyde undergoes reductive amination on the metal surface, then intramolecular condensation onto the second aldehyde and dehydration yields the cyclic imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step explicitly includes H2 and water, correcting the earlier chemically broken scheme in which succinaldehyde and NH3 were written to give 1-pyrroline without redox bookkeeping. The added reductant is essential: without it, a 1,4-dicarbonyl plus ammonia is better viewed as a pyrrole-forming system than a clean proline precursor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination of succinaldehyde to 4-aminobutanal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiS\", \"FeS\", \"native Fe\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiS/FeS/native Fe\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"H2-rich geothermal pool; moderate dilution helps retain the open-chain amino aldehyde before full cyclization.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-mediated reductive amination of one aldehyde function converts the dialdehyde into the amino aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the stepwise counterpart to rxn_005 and is fully mass-balanced because H2 and H2O are explicit. It is useful mechanistically because 4-aminobutanal is the direct precursor that spontaneously cyclizes to the C4 hub 1-pyrroline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Carboxylation of 1-pyrroline to \u03941-pyrroline-2-carboxylate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe2+\", \"carbonate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+/carbonate surface\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Alkaline bicarbonate-rich pool; elevated dissolved CO2/carbonate; low-yield but persistent cycling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The cyclic imine tautomerizes to an enamine-like nucleophile that traps CO2 at C2 to form the carboxylated imine acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the most speculative carbon-extension step in the network, but it is fully balanced and chemically sensible because cyclic imines can display enamine-like C2 nucleophilicity. It is included as the noncyanide alternative for installing the fifth carbon.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogenation of \u03941-pyrroline-2-carboxylate to DL-proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiS\", \"FeS\", \"native Fe\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiS/FeS/native Fe\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"H2-rich geothermal seep; reduction on an achiral surface gives racemate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogen adds across the cyclic C=N bond to generate the saturated pyrrolidine ring and the proline stereocenter.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the clean reduction step that earlier versions lacked. H2 is explicit, mass is conserved, and the product is correctly treated as DL-proline because achiral mineral hydrogenation should not strongly favor one enantiomer.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker capture of 1-pyrroline to pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cyanide-bearing pool, mildly acidic to neutral so the cyclic imine remains electrophilic while CN- is still available.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds across the C=N bond of the cyclic imine to give the saturated aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the cleaner way to formulate the Strecker branch. Instead of the earlier problematic direct dialdehyde-plus-HCN shortcut, the network first forms the cyclic imine and then performs a standard cyanide capture, which is both chemically clearer and stoichiometrically correct.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile to DL-proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Hot aqueous wet-dry cycling; pH swings during evaporation and rehydration accelerate nitrile hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The nitrile is hydrated and ultimately hydrolyzed to the carboxylic acid, liberating ammonia overall.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Water and NH3 are explicit here, fixing the bookkeeping problem that often appears in aminonitrile-to-amino-acid steps. The overall chemistry is standard nitrile hydrolysis and gives racemic proline because stereochemistry was set nonselectively in rxn_009.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"One-pot Strecker capture and hydrolysis of 1-pyrroline to DL-proline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Net one-pool route under hot aqueous cycling; the aminonitrile intermediate need not be isolated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN first traps the cyclic imine as an aminonitrile, which is then hydrolyzed in the same pool to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the condensed overall alternative to rxn_009 plus rxn_010. It is included because prebiotic pools could plausibly run both substeps in sequence without isolating pyrrolidine-2-carbonitrile, and the reaction explicitly includes the required 2 H2O equivalent overall and NH3 release.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Mineral-assisted resolution of DL-proline to an isolated L-proline fraction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"quartz\", \"calcite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Repeated dissolution/recrystallization or selective adsorption in evaporitic brines; explicit resolution only, with partial recovery of the L-enriched fraction rather than full conversion of the racemate.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Physical separation through differential adsorption/crystallization enriches one enantiomer and isolates the L fraction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This node is intentionally written as a resolution/enrichment step, not as an asymmetric synthesis. That addresses the stereochemical bottleneck directly: the preceding chemistry gives racemic proline, while repeated mineral-assisted separation can isolate an L-enriched recovered fraction.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Acrolein two-step source with CO2 carboxylation finish\",\n      \"description\": \"Acrolein is hydrocyanated and reduced to 4-aminobutanal, cyclized to 1-pyrroline, carboxylated with CO2 to P2C, then hydrogenated to racemic proline before explicit L-resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Acrolein two-step source with isolated aminonitrile hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The acrolein branch reaches 1-pyrroline, then uses Strecker capture to a cyclic aminonitrile followed by hydrolysis to racemic proline and final L-enrichment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Acrolein two-step source with one-pot Strecker-hydrolysis finish\",\n      \"description\": \"After forming 1-pyrroline from the acrolein branch, HCN capture and hydrolysis occur in the same hot pool to yield racemic proline, followed by L-resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct reductive cyclization of succinaldehyde with CO2 finish\",\n      \"description\": \"Succinaldehyde, ammonia, and H2 form 1-pyrroline directly on metal sulfides; CO2 carboxylation and hydrogenation then furnish racemic proline before L-resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct reductive cyclization of succinaldehyde with isolated aminonitrile hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The same direct succinaldehyde-to-1-pyrroline route is coupled to Strecker cyanide capture, separate nitrile hydrolysis, and explicit L-resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct reductive cyclization of succinaldehyde with one-pot Strecker-hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Succinaldehyde is first converted directly to 1-pyrroline, which then undergoes net cyanide capture and hydrolysis in one pool to racemic proline before L-resolution.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Stepwise succinaldehyde to 4-aminobutanal with CO2 finish\",\n      \"description\": \"Succinaldehyde is first reductively aminated to 4-aminobutanal, then cyclized to 1-pyrroline, carboxylated to P2C, hydrogenated to racemic proline, and finally resolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Stepwise succinaldehyde to 4-aminobutanal with isolated aminonitrile hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The stepwise succinaldehyde branch reaches 1-pyrroline through 4-aminobutanal, then uses the aminonitrile relay to racemic proline before L-enrichment.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Stepwise succinaldehyde to 4-aminobutanal with one-pot Strecker-hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Succinaldehyde is reductively aminated to 4-aminobutanal, cyclized to 1-py